          "source_quote": "καὶ εἰ ἀίδιος οὖσα περὶ ὧν ἔπραξεν ἐνταῦθα ἔχει κριθῆναι",
          "english_quote": "whether, if it is eternal, it is to be judged for what it has done here",
          "issue": "Taking ἀίδιος οὖσα conditionally as “if it is eternal” is defensible in the context of the preceding mortal-or-immortal question. However, the participle can also present eternity as the circumstance or premise of the judgment question: “whether, being eternal, it is to be judged.” This material difference is not disclosed. The existing note explains ἔχει κριθῆναι, not the conditional interpretation of the participle. This is interpretive ambiguity, not source corruption.",
